\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Does there exist an absolute constant $C$ such that every centered log-concave random vector $X\in\mathbb R^n$ satisfies $\operatorname{Var}(|X|^2)\leq C\lambda_X^2\mathbb E|X|^2$, where $\lambda_X^2$ is the largest eigenvalue of its covariance matrix?
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2012/59}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does there exist an absolute constant $C$ such that every centered log-concave random vector $X\in\mathbb R^n$ satisfies $\operatorname{Var}(|X|^2)\leq C\lambda_X^2\mathbb E|X|^2$, where $\lambda_X^2$ is the largest eigenvalue of its covariance matrix?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 30002247 (OWR-12182-002). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
